<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Percy Cox</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1864–1937</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Percy Cox</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Percy Cox was a British army officer and colonial administrator who spent most of his career in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. As civil commissioner and then High Commissioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1920 to 1923, he helped install Faisal I as king and set up the mandate government. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqair">Uqair</a> conference of 1922 he drew borders between Iraq, Kuwait and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/percy-cox.jpg" alt="Percy Cox" width="279" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, photo taken in 1916 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Percy_Cox_1916.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
